Let the two random numbers be $x$ and $y$. In order to form an obtuse triangle, since 1 will be the longest side, we must simultaneously satisfy the following inequalities: $$x+y>1\text{ and }x^2+y^2<1.$$The first is the triangle inequality and the second guarantees that the triangle is obtuse. Graphing these in the $xy$-plane, we get the following shaded region. [asy]
draw(unitsquare);
draw((0,0)--(1,0),EndArrow);
draw((0,0)--(0,1),EndArrow);
label("0",(0,0),SW);
label("1",(1,0),S);
label("1",(0,1),W);
label("$x$",(.5,0),S);
label("$y$",(0,.5),W);
fill((1,0)--(0,1)..(3/5,4/5)..cycle,gray(.7));
[/asy] The curve is an arc of the unit circle centered at the origin. This area is then equal to that sector minus the right isosceles triangle within it, or $\frac{\pi}{4}-\frac{1}{2}=\frac{4 - \pi}{4}.$ And since the area of the square is $1,$ $p = \frac{4 - \pi}{4}.$

Four times $p$ is $\boxed{4 - \pi}$.

By the Binomial Theorem, we have  \begin{align*}
301^4 &= (3(100) + 1)^4\\
&= \binom40 \cdot 3^4 \cdot 100^4 \cdot 1^0 + \binom41 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 100^3 \cdot 1^1 \\
&\qquad\qquad+ \binom42 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 100^2 \cdot 1^2+ \binom43 \cdot 3^1 \cdot 100^1 \cdot 1^3 \\
&\qquad\qquad+ \binom44 \cdot 3^0 \cdot 100^0 \cdot 1^4.
\end{align*} All but the last two terms are divisible by $10000=100^2$, so we only have to consider the remainder when the last two terms are divided by 10,000. The last two terms are  \begin{align*}
\binom43 &\cdot 3^1 \cdot 100^1 \cdot 1^3 + \binom44 \cdot 3^0 \cdot 100^0 \cdot 1^4\\
&= 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 100 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 1 \cdot 1 \cdot 1\\
& = 1200 + 1 = \boxed{9201}.\end{align*}

